CAT = "cisInspect"; SEG = "INS"
Q = [
('What is the main purpose of inspecting instruments after cleaning and before assembly?',
 'To find leftover soil or damage', ['To check the set is sterile', 'To confirm the washer ran', "To record the tray's weight"],
 "Inspection checks cleaning efficacy and the instrument's integrity. Residual bioburden blocks sterilization and damage can harm the patient."),
('FDA guidance says that if a device is not visually clean at the end of cleaning, the user should do what?',
 'Repeat cleaning or dispose of it', ['Sterilize it on a longer cycle', 'Wipe it with alcohol and pack it', 'Inspect it again once dry'],
 "FDA's 2015 reprocessing guidance says the instructions should tell users to repeat the relevant cleaning steps or safely dispose of a device that is not visually clean."),
('Which two things does FDA guidance say visual inspection may need to see fine soil and defects?',
 'Magnification and lighting', ['A swab and a pH strip', 'A torque gauge and a ruler', 'A white towel and gloves'],
 'FDA says visual inspection instructions may include magnification and adequate lighting. Lighted magnifiers help find soil in serrations and hinges.'),
('Which tool is used to look for blood or tissue inside a long lumen that the eye cannot see into?',
 'A borescope', ['An insulation tester', 'A magnifying lamp', 'A continuity tester'],
 "A borescope's thin probe carries a camera or optics down the lumen. A magnifying lamp only helps with surfaces you can see directly."),
('What can a white inspection swab show when wiped through a cleaned box lock?',
 'Hidden residual soil', ['Whether the steel is magnetic', 'Whether the set is sterile', 'Whether the ratchet holds'],
 'A swab drawn through a joint or lumen picks up soil that a quick look misses. Any staining on it means the item goes back for cleaning.'),
('FDA guidance lists which signs of deterioration as reasons a reusable device fails inspection?',
 'Corrosion, pitting, cracked seals', ['Fingerprints, dust and lint', 'Loose labels and torn wrappers', 'Satin finish and dull handles'],
 "FDA's guidance gives corrosion, discoloration, pitting and cracked seals as examples of unacceptable deterioration in acceptance or failure criteria."),
('Thumb forceps stay partly closed after the technician lets go. What does this show?',
 'Weak spring tension', ['Normal design for forceps', 'Too much lubricant', 'A worn ratchet'],
 'Thumb forceps should spring fully open when released. Weak spring tension makes them hard to control, so they are sent for repair. Thumb forceps have no ratchet.'),
('A brown spot on a clamp is rubbed with a pencil eraser and leaves a small pit in the steel. What should happen?',
 'Pull it for evaluation', ['Polish it and return it to the set', 'Coat it with extra lubricant', 'Re-sterilize it twice'],
 'A pit under the spot means true corrosion. Corroded instruments can spread rust to others and cannot be cleaned reliably, so they are pulled for repair or replacement.'),
('A rainbow or blue-gold tint appears on stainless steel after repeated steam cycles. What usually causes it?',
 'Heat tint on the oxide layer', ['Blood left in the box lock', 'Laser marking fading away', 'Dissolved tungsten carbide'],
 'Heat tint is a thin colored oxide that forms with heat. It is usually cosmetic, but the outline asks staff to tell staining apart from rust and damage.'),
('Why is a burr on the tip of a tissue forceps a problem?',
 'It can snag and tear tissue', ['It shows the tips are aligned', 'It holds the jaws apart', 'It is normal after sharpening'],
 'A burr is a sharp rough spot that catches gauze or tissue. Running the tips gently over gauze or a cotton ball shows a burr when fibers snag.'),
('How is the jaw alignment of a ring-handled clamp checked?',
 'Close it and check the tips meet', ['Open it and check the box lock', 'Close it on gauze and pull', 'Check the ratchet teeth by eye'],
 'When the jaws close, tips and serrations should meet evenly with no gaps, crossing or overlap. Misaligned jaws do not hold tissue securely.'),
('What should be checked along the full length of scissor blades?',
 'Smooth action and a clean cut', ['Even color along each edge', 'A tight, stiff box lock', 'Tips that cross before closing'],
 'Scissors should open and close smoothly, and the blades should cut cleanly all the way to the tips with no catching or folding of the test material.'),
('Why are test materials used instead of gauze to check scissor sharpness?',
 'They give a repeatable test', ['Gauze is too expensive to use', 'Gauze dulls the blades', 'Gauze blocks the hinge'],
 "Purpose-made test materials have a set thickness and texture, so results are repeatable. The scissor size and maker's guidance decide which material is used."),
('How should test media be chosen for a cutting instrument?',
 'As its manufacturer recommends', ['The thinnest material on hand', 'Gauze folded four layers', 'The same for all scissors'],
 'The outline lists test tools, materials and rejection criteria. Manufacturers say which media and method show their instrument cuts as designed.'),
('Why can visual inspection alone miss insulation damage on laparoscopic instruments?',
 'Pinholes can be too small to see', ['Insulation is always transparent', 'Damage only shows when wet', 'Insulation changes color daily'],
 'Very small breaks in insulation are often invisible, which is why insulation testers are used. Studies of electrosurgical instruments found more failures with a tester than by eye.'),
("Why is a break in a laparoscopic instrument's insulation dangerous?",
 'Current can burn tissue unseen', ['The jaws stop opening fully', 'The jaws heat up too slowly', 'Steam cannot reach the shaft'],
 "Electrosurgical current can escape through the break and burn tissue away from the working tip, often outside the camera's view."),
('A suction tip has a small dent in its shaft. Why does it fail inspection?',
 'It can block flow and hold soil', ['It only matters on long tips', 'It is cosmetic if it suctions', 'It weakens the thumb port seal'],
 'A dent narrows the lumen, which reduces suction and creates a spot where soil collects and brushing cannot reach.'),
("A self-retaining retractor's ratchet slips under light pressure. What does this mean?",
 'It cannot stay open safely', ['It needs more lubricant only', 'It is fine for light tissue', 'It needs its tension reset'],
 'A self-retaining retractor depends on its ratchet to stay open. If it slips, it can close on tissue during the case, so it goes for repair.'),
('Besides its outer surfaces, which part of a cannulated drill bit must be checked after cleaning?',
 'The cannula, end to end', ['The laser mark on the shank', 'The coating on the flutes', 'The fit in the drill chuck'],
 'Cannulated instruments have a lumen that must be clear of bone and soil. A stylet, brush or borescope confirms it is open and clean.'),
("During assembly a technician finds an instrument not listed on the set's count sheet. What is the best step?",
 'Identify it and return it', ['Leave it in the set it is in', 'Throw it away as waste', 'Add it to the sheet by hand'],
 'An extra item may belong to another set, which then goes out short. Identifying it with the catalog and returning it keeps both counts accurate.'),
('Why should a hinged instrument be opened fully when it is inspected?',
 'Soil hides in the box lock', ['It shows the ratchet works', 'It tests the spring tension', 'It lets the joint cool down'],
 'Blood and tissue collect in the box lock and between serrations, which can only be seen with the jaws open under magnification.'),
("A set's stringer holds ring-handled instruments. What is checked when loading it?",
 'Rings open and not crowded', ['Every ring is fully closed', 'All tips point the same way only', 'The heaviest items are on top'],
 'Stringers keep ring-handled instruments open and organized. Overloading crowds the joints and blocks sterilant, and heavy items go at the bottom of a tray.'),
('Why are heavy instruments placed at the bottom of a tray during assembly?',
 'To avoid crushing fine items', ['To help the tray drain faster', 'To speed up the count', 'To lower the cycle time'],
 'Placing heavy retractors and mallets low keeps them from shifting onto fine instruments and bending tips during handling.'),
('Why is a delicate ophthalmic instrument often placed in a protective holder or mat in its tray?',
 'To keep the tips from damage', ['To keep it apart for counting', 'To speed drying of the tips', 'To mark it for the surgeon'],
 'Fine tips bend or break easily. Silicone mats and holders keep instruments apart and still during processing and transport.'),
('A technician finds a cracked tooth on the jaw of a tissue forceps. What is the right step?',
 'Pull it for repair or replacement', ['File the crack smooth and reuse it', 'Tape over the crack', 'Put it in the next set as a spare'],
 'Cracked or broken teeth can tear tissue or break off in the patient. The instrument goes out of service until repaired or replaced.'),
('When an instrument is checked for integrity and functionality, what is confirmed?',
 'It is intact and works', ['It is clean enough', 'It is in its set', 'It is sharp'],
 'Integrity means no cracks, missing parts or damage; functionality means it opens, closes, cuts or holds as intended. Cleanliness is a separate check.'),
('Why is the full count of a set checked against the count sheet before wrapping?',
 'So the OR gets every item', ['So the wrap size is right', 'So the tray stays balanced', 'So tracking logs a scan'],
 'A complete, correct set prevents delays and wrong substitutions in the OR. Missing items are documented and the OR is told.'),
('Why does FDA guidance urge caution with oil-based and silicone lubricants on reusable devices?',
 'They can shield microbes', ['They turn the steel blue', 'They dull cutting edges', 'They stain the box locks'],
 'FDA warns that these lubricants may coat and protect surface microorganisms and reduce the effectiveness of steam and EO sterilization. The IFU names a compatible lubricant.'),
]
